{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChG2me5ebepay4LgQf7Ba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0136GzBF4VaFojG3dp6LSUYk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"PY=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-brenn-dev-brace/255658ca-f19b-460c-8892-2a477de97733/scratchpad/venv/bin/python; $PY skill/scripts/review_coverage.py inventory agent 2>&1 | tail -20; ls agent; awk '/^## R04/,/^## R09/' skill/references/run-time.md | grep -v '^$'"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5182,"cache_read_input_tokens":21182,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5182},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ed5773-a566-47b2-9286-6c65525da62e","uuid":"6d622b9e-1633-45e6-a6cb-efd014f3391d","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:56:31.026Z","request_id":"req_011ChG2mdvihxoP3sfKSSBnE","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0136GzBF4VaFojG3dp6LSUYk":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-hses_u_o && PY=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-brenn-dev-brace/255658ca-f19b-460c-8892-2a477de97733/scratchpad/venv/bin/python; $PY skill/scripts/review_coverage.py inventory agent 2>&1 | tail -20; ls agent; awk '/^## R04/,/^## R09/' skill/references/run-time.md | grep -v '^$'"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_0136GzBF4VaFojG3dp6LSUYk":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-hses_u_o"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0136GzBF4VaFojG3dp6LSUYk","type":"tool_result","content":"Wrote agent/brace-inventory.json; candidate matches need manual scope review.\nbrace-findings.json\nbrace-inventory.json\nbrace-prescan.json\nCODEOWNERS\nconfig\nDockerfile\nk8s\nmigrations\nprompts\nREADME.md\nrelease\nrequirements.txt\nsrc\n## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n### Requirement\nThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n### Verification recipe\nCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n**Red flags:**\n- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n**Where to look:**\n- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n- Cache layers in front of search\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n### Requirement\nSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n### Verification recipe\nIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n**Red flags:**\n- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n- Memory entries that never expire.\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n- Memory write tools\n- Session and thread handling\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n### Requirement\nFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n### Verification recipe\nSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n**Red flags:**\n- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory schema or data model\n- Summarization and compaction code\n- Admin or cleanup scripts\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n### Requirement\nFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n### Verification recipe\nCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory every model call, including classifiers and helpers, and every action/audit write. 2) Trace available decision-time context into each record. 3) Answer no when an available measure is dropped; NULL/unavailable is not permission to omit a value that exists. Do not exclude routing decisions or delegate start/result events merely by calling them lifecycle events.\n- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory parent-to-child calls, tool calls, child returns and their audit records. 2) Trace the producer, call arguments, callee and result record for each path. 3) Answer no when available parent or child context is dropped on either direction; correct logging inside one leaf tool is not proof of handoff coverage.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n**Red flags:**\n- No token or context-size field on action records.\n- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n- Compaction or summarization code\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n**N/A when:** never\n## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n### Requirement\nKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n### Verification recipe\nSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n**Red flags:**\n- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n- Alerting and on-call routing config\n- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n**N/A when:** never\n## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ed5773-a566-47b2-9286-6c65525da62e","uuid":"e6fac7d1-7802-4a56-bae6-dfeaa281f82f","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:56:31.324Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Wrote agent/brace-inventory.json; candidate matches need manual scope review.\nbrace-findings.json\nbrace-inventory.json\nbrace-prescan.json\nCODEOWNERS\nconfig\nDockerfile\nk8s\nmigrations\nprompts\nREADME.md\nrelease\nrequirements.txt\nsrc\n## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n### Requirement\nThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n### Verification recipe\nCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n**Red flags:**\n- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n**Where to look:**\n- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n- Cache layers in front of search\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n### Requirement\nSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n### Verification recipe\nIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n**Red flags:**\n- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n- Memory entries that never expire.\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n- Memory write tools\n- Session and thread handling\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n### Requirement\nFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n### Verification recipe\nSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n**Red flags:**\n- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory schema or data model\n- Summarization and compaction code\n- Admin or cleanup scripts\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n### Requirement\nFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n### Verification recipe\nCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory every model call, including classifiers and helpers, and every action/audit write. 2) Trace available decision-time context into each record. 3) Answer no when an available measure is dropped; NULL/unavailable is not permission to omit a value that exists. Do not exclude routing decisions or delegate start/result events merely by calling them lifecycle events.\n- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory parent-to-child calls, tool calls, child returns and their audit records. 2) Trace the producer, call arguments, callee and result record for each path. 3) Answer no when available parent or child context is dropped on either direction; correct logging inside one leaf tool is not proof of handoff coverage.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n**Red flags:**\n- No token or context-size field on action records.\n- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n- Compaction or summarization code\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n**N/A when:** never\n## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n### Requirement\nKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n### Verification recipe\nSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n### Checks for likely_pass\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n### Not covered here\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n### Heuristics\n**Provable from code:** partly\n**Look for:**\n- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n**Red flags:**\n- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n- Alerting and on-call routing config\n- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n**N/A when:** never\n## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0136GzBF4VaFojG3dp6LSUYk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
